WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.302
You said recently in

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:02.902
a speech

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:03.603
that if

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.839
we keep having these terrible differences

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:08.475
that we have, we will destroy each other.

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:09.776
We have to find a way

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:12.178
how to live together.

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:13.680
I spoke to one of the Republican

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.881
candidates, former Governor

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.816
Asa Hutchinson of Arkansas,

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:18.051
who said to me,

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:19.386
give the candidates a chance

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:20.453
to talk about the issues

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.255
that Americans are concerned about.

00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:23.890
Let's use appropriate language.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:24.691
Let's be clear

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:26.593
that we have differences of policy,

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:27.660
but that doesn't always make

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.762
the person on the other side

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:30.363
an evil person or somebody

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:32.966
that doesn't love our country.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.101
Do you think the Republicans

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:35.802
will coalesce

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:36.936
around that kind of message?

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:37.437
No.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:42.042
There's no evidence that that's where

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:43.309
their head is at right now.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:45.378
Now, that doesn't mean that that's

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.614
not attainable over time.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:51.518
You know, look, I.

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:52.185
It wasn't

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:53.086
that long ago

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:55.622
that I got a lot of Republican votes.

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:58.091
It wasn't that long ago where

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:01.361
John McCain was the Republican nominee

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:05.799
and actively shut down

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:08.001
a speaker at a town hall

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:10.270
who was saying that I was an

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:14.741
illegal alien bent on imposing

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:18.411
Sharia law on the United States.

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:21.181
And there are still a bunch of folks

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:24.050
who are more politically conservative

00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:26.619
than I am on social issues, on

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:29.622
economic issues, but who I consider

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.525
good people, thoughtful people

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:34.127
who I learn from

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:36.096
and who I enjoy conversations with.

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:41.000
And so the polarization

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:41.901
that we've seen in

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.937
our national politics is not

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:46.272
identical to

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.807
what's happening on the ground.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:50.743
But what is true is that partly

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:53.313
because of

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:54.848
where people are getting information

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:55.682
these days,

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:58.618
the siloing of of information.

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.553
If you are watching Fox News,

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:01.387
I've said this before,

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:03.356
if you're watching Fox News or

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:08.061
following on some right wing radio host

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.297
or getting Facebook feeds

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:13.199
within that bubble,

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:14.701
your reality is different

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:15.635
than if you read The New

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:16.736
York Times or watch

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:18.671
your program.

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:19.772
And

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.242
when when people are getting such

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:25.245
fundamentally different facts

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:26.446
or what they think

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:27.080
to be facts

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.450
and their worldviews are so skewed

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.418
and in a

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.120
one direction or another,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.489
and it's very hard for democracy to work.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.757
So there's the reason

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.125
why I've been spending a lot of time,

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:42.061
both in the foundation and in other work,

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:43.930
talking about these problems

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.598
of misinformation,

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:47.200
not just the kind of misinformation

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.302
that we see Putin engaging in,

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:53.373
in in the Ukraine situation, not just

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:55.975
during election time,

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:58.878
but just this constant demonization

00:02:58.878 --> 00:02:59.712
of the other side,

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:02.081
making people fearful of each other.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:02.882
And unfortunately,

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.417
I think that's going to be

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:07.487
a problem that gets even more pronounced

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:08.988
with the advent of A.I.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:11.191
and deepfakes and all these challenges.

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:12.859
And we want to talk about that a lot with

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:13.893
with the leaders

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.061
in the second part of this program.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:15.962
And I just wanted to ask you

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:18.231
before I got to them finally race.

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:21.301
You're the first black president

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:22.902
when Trump was elected

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:24.003
somebody who used to work for you

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:25.171
and now is an analyst.

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:26.940
Van Jones said white lash.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.108
It was the white lash against

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.476
a black presidency.

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:33.246
Do you think the white lashes receding?

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.448
And I guess combined with that,

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:37.650
how do you interpret two candidates

00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:38.818
of color?

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:40.053
Nikki Haley,

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:40.920
former governor of South

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:41.621
Carolina, Tim

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:43.790
Scott, Senator of South Carolina,

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.593
who is saying that Obama wants to keep

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:49.529
essentially race as part of the

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:50.763
of the equation,

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:51.431
a part of the

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:52.765
you know, the conversation again.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.033
And you have

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.235
you don't believe that

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:56.970
that everybody has an equal chance

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:57.770
in the United States

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.505
no matter what their color?

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:00.773
Well, look,

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:02.208
I won't comment on

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:04.244
what Republican candidates say.

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:05.478
I'm not running. So they can

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:08.715
say, hey, they can

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:11.384
find other ways to occupy their time

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.222
I think race has always been

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:18.224
the fault line in American

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:19.826
life, in American politics.

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:21.060
That's not original to me.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.829
I think any observer of America

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:23.963
would say that

00:04:25.999 --> 00:04:27.133
I and by the way,

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.569
that historically has not sort of been

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:32.672
a one sided partizan issue.

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:36.142
My favorite president, Abraham Lincoln,

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:37.877
did an awful lot

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.579
to advance the cause of freedom.

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:41.914
And conversely,

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:43.783
the Democratic Party was

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:47.053
where the Dixiecrats resisted

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.623
civil rights and progress for years

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:52.458
and imposed Jim Crow.

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:53.526
So

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:57.096
it is something that

00:04:57.497 --> 00:04:58.831
America has had to grapple

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:01.701
with for centuries.

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:04.170
I think we have made real progress and,

00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:06.105
you know,

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:07.940
although I was always skeptical

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:08.641
that by election

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:10.610
somehow signified a post-racial America.

00:05:11.878 --> 00:05:13.279
If you look at any speech

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:14.881
I gave throughout my presidency,

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:16.082
I was always someone

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:18.051
who reminded the country of the progress

00:05:18.051 --> 00:05:19.619
that was possible.

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:21.354
That was my brand.

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:24.223
That's part of the hope and change thing.

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:26.693
But what I've also always said about

00:05:26.693 --> 00:05:29.195
hope was it can't be blind hope.

00:05:29.262 --> 00:05:32.665
It can't be a willful ignorance

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:33.733
to our history.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:36.369
We reckon with our history.

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:40.173
That's how we then

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:41.407
get better.

00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:43.242
That's how we perfect our union.

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:43.509
You know,

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:44.177
in the same way

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:46.112
that Germany got better

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:47.680
when it looked squarely

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:48.981
at what happened

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:49.549
during World

00:05:49.549 --> 00:05:51.551
War Two and came to terms with that.

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:52.952
And that's part of why

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:53.786
it is a

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:55.188
thriving, stable

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:57.256
and increasingly diverse society.

00:05:57.857 --> 00:05:59.125
And, you know,

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:00.927
that's part of the argument that I think

00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:03.930
all of us,

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:04.497
not just in the

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:05.331
United States

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:06.032
or in Europe,

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:06.766
but around the world,

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:08.868
have to come to terms with

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:11.304
humans, have a strong desire

00:06:11.304 --> 00:06:12.271
to coalesce,

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:13.005
particularly during

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:15.708
times of stress around tribe,

00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:19.512
clan, race,

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:20.980
you know,

00:06:21.514 --> 00:06:23.883
whatever our religious preferences are.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:26.319
And politicians

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:27.954
have a good way of exploiting that.

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:30.256
And if we don't resist it,

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:31.858
then we're going to have problems.

00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:32.558
And by the way, it's

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:33.960
not just that

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:34.994
that us them

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:37.063
dynamic is not just around race.

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:39.031
I would argue that in the United States

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:40.299
and I suspect in Europe

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:42.635
as well, changing gender roles

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:45.705
have have fueled at least

00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:47.073
as much of a backlash

00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:48.374
as the racial backlash

00:06:48.374 --> 00:06:51.377
that's this enormous fear

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:52.512
among

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:56.416
men and of those

00:06:56.416 --> 00:06:59.452
who like the traditional structures

00:06:59.452 --> 00:07:01.187
and hierarchies and patriarchy

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:03.122
get very

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:05.625
nervous when you have women suddenly

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:07.026
being outspoken

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:08.528
in thinking that they should have

00:07:08.528 --> 00:07:10.563
the same rights and power as men do.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:11.831
And when you have people

00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:13.866
of different sexual orientation saying,

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:14.700
I'm here,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.235
I want a seat at the table,

00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:18.871
that has been very threatening.

00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:20.173
And there's one last ingredient

00:07:20.173 --> 00:07:22.642
that I'd be remiss if I didn't mention.

00:07:23.443 --> 00:07:26.412
I do think there's an economic

00:07:26.412 --> 00:07:29.048
element to our democracy

00:07:29.048 --> 00:07:31.384
that we have to pay attention to.

00:07:31.384 --> 00:07:33.753
Our democracy is not going to be healthy

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:36.122
with the levels of inequality

00:07:36.122 --> 00:07:38.090
that we've seen generated

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.626
from globalization, automation,

00:07:41.928 --> 00:07:44.564
the decline in unions,

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:47.600
obscene inequality.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.036
You think about news of the day.

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:51.537
Generally, we're not talking

00:07:51.537 --> 00:07:52.238
about news of the day,

00:07:52.238 --> 00:07:54.106
but right now we have

00:07:54.106 --> 00:07:55.875
a 20 for our coverage,

00:07:57.043 --> 00:07:57.343
and I

00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:59.879
understand it, of this submarine,

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.748
the submersible

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:05.651
that that tragically is right now

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:08.588
we've lost at the bottom of the sea

00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:12.458
at the same time right here

00:08:12.458 --> 00:08:15.027
in just off the coast of Greece,

00:08:15.061 --> 00:08:17.797
we had 700

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.433
people dead, several hundred migrants

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:23.035
who were apparently being smuggled

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:25.171
into here.

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:28.774
And we've made news, but it's not

00:08:29.308 --> 00:08:30.810
dominating in the same way.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:31.911
And in some ways

00:08:31.911 --> 00:08:34.614
it's indicative of the degree to which

00:08:35.548 --> 00:08:37.483
people's life chances

00:08:37.483 --> 00:08:40.753
have have grown so desperate.

00:08:40.753 --> 00:08:42.989
It's very hard to sustain a democracy

00:08:43.422 --> 00:08:45.391
when you have such massive

00:08:45.391 --> 00:08:46.526
concentrations of wealth.

00:08:46.526 --> 00:08:50.897
And so part of my argument has been that

00:08:51.664 --> 00:08:53.733
unless we attend to that,

00:08:53.733 --> 00:08:55.401
unless we

00:08:55.401 --> 00:08:56.135
make people feel

00:08:56.135 --> 00:08:58.971
more economically secure, and we're in

00:08:59.972 --> 00:09:01.340
taking more seriously

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.342
the need to create ladders of opportunity

00:09:03.342 --> 00:09:05.011
and a stronger safety net

00:09:05.011 --> 00:09:07.313
that's adapted to these new technologies

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:08.948
and the displacements

00:09:08.948 --> 00:09:10.249
that are taking place around the world,

00:09:10.249 --> 00:09:11.551
if we don't take care of that,

00:09:12.618 --> 00:09:12.985
that's

00:09:12.985 --> 00:09:15.054
also going to fuel the kind of

00:09:16.122 --> 00:09:18.524
mostly far right populism But it can also

00:09:19.091 --> 00:09:20.893
potentially come from the left

00:09:20.893 --> 00:09:23.329
that is undermining democracy because

00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:25.231
it makes people angry and

00:09:25.231 --> 00:09:26.332
resentful and scared.

